{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012CMkoteKtSTmBDJBQEHqg9","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Voice assistant: statement and debt services, service contract\n2\t\n3\tAuthor: Mert Koç, Integration Architecture, Freya Bank (mert.koc@freyabank.example)\n4\tDate: 1 October 2026\n5\tDistribution: Selin Aydın, Kerem Yalçın, Burcu Tan (Freya Bank); Deniz Arı (Freya)\n6\t\n7\t> Mert: This is the contract the voice assistant integrates against for the statement / debt use case. It covers only the seven services the assistant calls. Everything about how the call reaches the assistant and how it goes back to the phone menu is Burcu's side and is not in here. If something below disagrees with what you see in the test environment, tell me and I'll check which side is wrong.\n8\t\n9\t## 1. Before you start\n10\t\n11\t- The caller is already identified when the assistant picks up. The phone menu hands over the **customer number** with the call, so there is no customer search service in this contract. Start from `cards.list`.\n12\t- Every service is `POST`, JSON in, JSON out, UTF-8.\n13\t- Base address for all seven:\n14\t\n15\t```\n16\thttps://api.freyabank.example/voice/v1/\n17\t```\n18\t\n19\t  So the card list is `POST https://api.freyabank.example/voice/v1/cards.list`, and so on.\n20\t- Dates are strings in `dd.MM.yyyy` (e.g. `\"05.11.2026\"`). Amounts are JSON numbers with a dot as decimal separator (`5000.13`), never formatted strings. Do not round them.\n21\t- Turning numbers and dates into speech is the assistant's job. The services return raw values only, no sentences meant to be read to the caller (the `messageText` in errors is for logs, please don't read it out).\n22\t\n23\t## 2. Access token\n24\t\n25\tThe services sit behind our API gateway. You need a bearer token first.\n26\t\n27\t```\n28\tPOST https://api.freyabank.example/oauth2/token\n29\tContent-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded\n30\t\n31\tgrant_type=client_credentials&client_id=<client id>&client_secret=<client secret>&scope=voice.statement\n32\t```\n33\t\n34\tAnswer:\n35\t\n36\t```json\n37\t{\n38\t  \"access_token\": \"<token>\",\n39\t  \"token_type\": \"Bearer\",\n40\t  \"expires_in\": 1800\n41\t}\n42\t```\n43\t\n44\tThen on every service call:\n45\t\n46\t| Header | Value |\n47\t|---|---|\n48\t| `Authorization` | `Bearer <token>` |\n49\t| `Content-Type` | `application/json` |\n50\t| `Accept` | `application/json` |\n51\t\n52\tThe token belongs to the integration (service account), not to the caller. Cache it and get a fresh one when it runs out; don't fetch one per call. Client id and secret: I'll send them over the secure channel, not by e-mail.\n53\t\n54\t(Test environment: the token and headers are not checked there. Send them anyway so nothing changes when we switch.)\n55\t\n56\t## 3. Envelope\n57\t\n58\t### Request\n59\t\n60\t```json\n61\t{\n62\t  \"data\": { \"...service fields...\" },\n63\t  \"call\": { \"ani\": \"<caller's number>\", \"lang\": \"tr\" }\n64\t}\n65\t```\n66\t\n67\t`data` carries the fields of each service (section 5). `call` is call metadata we log on our side; nothing in this contract depends on it and the test environment ignores it.\n68\t\n69\t### Response\n70\t\n71\tEvery answer has a `result` object. Successful answers also have `data`.\n72\t\n73\t```json\n74\t{\n75\t  \"data\": { \"...service fields...\" },\n76\t  \"result\": {\n77\t    \"guid\": \"f0e1d2c3b4a5968778695a4b3c2d0002\",\n78\t    \"returnCode\": \"1\",\n79\t    \"messageText\": \"Success\",\n80\t    \"code\": \"1\",\n81\t    \"message\": \"Success\",\n82\t    \"type\": null,\n83\t    \"details\": {\n84\t      \"guid\": \"f0e1d2c3b4a5968778695a4b3c2d0002\",\n85\t      \"rootCause\": null,\n86\t      \"errorOrigin\": null\n87\t    }\n88\t  }\n89\t}\n90\t```\n91\t\n92\tSuccess = HTTP 200 and `result.returnCode` is `\"1\"`. Anything else is an error, see section 4. `guid` is our trace id; if you open a ticket with us, give us the `guid`.\n93\t\n94\t## 4. Errors\n95\t\n96\tError answers have **no `data` object**, only `result`:\n97\t\n98\t```json\n99\t{\n100\t  \"result\": {\n101\t    \"guid\": \"f0e1d2c3b4a5968778695a4b3c2d0086\",\n102\t    \"returnCode\": \"FBE00099\",\n103\t    \"messageText\": \"Teknik hata: ABORTED: \",\n104\t    \"code\": \"FBE00099\",\n105\t    \"message\": \"Teknik hata: ABORTED: \",\n106\t    \"type\": \"ERROR\",\n107\t    \"details\": {\n108\t      \"guid\": \"f0e1d2c3b4a5968778695a4b3c2d0086\",\n109\t      \"rootCause\": \"TechnicalError\",\n110\t      \"errorOrigin\": \"ivr-gateway-test:ivr-channel-v0\"\n111\t    }\n112\t  }\n113\t}\n114\t```\n115\t\n116\t`returnCode` and `code` always carry the same value, and so do `messageText` and `message`. `type` is `\"ERROR\"` or `\"WARNING\"`. `rootCause` and `errorOrigin` are diagnostic strings; log them, don't branch on them.\n117\t\n118\tCodes you can get:\n119\t\n120\t| HTTP | `code` | `type` | When | Services |\n121\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n122\t| 400 | `INVALID_ARGUMENTS_ERROR` | `ERROR` | A required field is missing or empty (message names the field, e.g. `\"card number cannot be empty\"`) | all |\n123\t| 401 | (gateway) | | Token missing or expired. Get a new token and retry once | all |\n124\t| 404 | `FB_IVR_CHANNEL_102` | `WARNING` | Customer has no valid card (message: `\"Müşteriye ait geçerli kart bilgisi bulunamadı.\"`) | `cards.list` |\n125\t| 404 | `FB_IVR_CHANNEL_120` | `WARNING` | Card not found | `cards.balance` |\n126\t| 404 | `FB_IVR_CHANNEL_121` | `WARNING` | Card not found | `statement.week.get` |\n127\t| 404 | `FB_IVR_CHANNEL_107` | `WARNING` | No statement document to send (message: `\"Pdf dokümanı bulunamadı\"`) | `statement.pdf.send` |\n128\t| 500 | `FBE00099` | `ERROR` | Technical error on our side (message: `\"Teknik hata: ABORTED: \"`) | all |\n129\t\n130\tNote on the `cards.list` 404: it's a WARNING, it means \"this customer has no card the assistant can work with\", not that the service is broken. How the assistant reacts to that vs. a 500 is for Selin's analysis, I'm only flagging that they are different things.\n131\t\n132\tTimeouts: we haven't agreed a client timeout yet. Most answers are well under a second, but under load (and once in the test data) an answer can take longer than ten seconds and still succeed. Open point, see section 7.\n133\t\n134\t## 5. Services\n135\t\n136\t| # | Service | Called with | Gives back |\n137\t|---|---|---|---|\n138\t| 5.1 | `cards.list` | customer number | the customer's cards the assistant may talk about |\n139\t| 5.2 | `cards.balance` | card number | statement and due dates, debt per currency |\n140\t| 5.3 | `statement.week.get` | card number | the card's current statement week and day, joint statement info |\n141\t| 5.4 | `statement.week.decide` | card number + requested week | what the dates *would* be with that week (does not change anything) |\n142\t| 5.5 | `statement.week.change` | card number + requested week | changes the statement week |\n143\t| 5.6 | `contact.email.get` | customer number | registered e-mail / phone |\n144\t| 5.7 | `statement.pdf.send` | customer, card, currency, statement date, address | sends the statement PDF by e-mail |\n145\t\n146\tCard number: always send the `cardNumber` **exactly as `cards.list` returned it**. Don't rebuild it from digits the caller says.\n147\t\n148\t### 5.1 `cards.list`\n149\t\n150\tRequest:\n151\t\n152\t```json\n153\t{\n154\t  \"data\": { \"customerNo\": \"90000001\", \"aiFlow\": \"CardOps\" },\n155\t  \"call\": { \"ani\": \"<caller's number>\", \"lang\": \"tr\" }\n156\t}\n157\t```\n158\t\n159\t`customerNo` may be sent as a string or as a number, both are accepted. `aiFlow` tells us which use case is asking; for this one it's `\"CardOps\"` (not validated in test).\n160\t\n161\tResponse, one card:\n162\t\n163\t```json\n164\t{\n165\t  \"data\": {\n166\t    \"cards\": [\n167\t      {\n168\t        \"cardNumber\": \"540000******1234\",\n169\t        \"cardNumberLast4\": \"1234\",\n170\t        \"productName\": \"Yıldız Pratik Classic\",\n171\t        \"status\": \"A\",\n172\t        \"isMainCard\": true\n173\t      }\n174\t    ],\n175\t    \"validCardsCount\": 1,\n176\t    \"exitState\": \"1_CARD\"\n177\t  },\n178\t  \"result\": { \"returnCode\": \"1\", \"...\": \"as in section 3\" }\n179\t}\n180\t```\n181\t\n182\tResponse, several cards (abridged):\n183\t\n184\t```json\n185\t{\n186\t  \"data\": {\n187\t    \"cards\": [\n188\t      { \"cardNumber\": \"540000******4253\", \"cardNumberLast4\": \"4253\", \"productName\": \"Yıldız Pratik\", \"status\": \"A\", \"isMainCard\": true },\n189\t      { \"cardNumber\": \"545000******4253\", \"cardNumberLast4\": \"4253\", \"productName\": \"Yıldız Pratik\", \"status\": \"A\", \"isMainCard\": true },\n190\t      { \"cardNumber\": \"540000******6184\", \"cardNumberLast4\": \"6184\", \"productName\": \"Yıldız Pratik\", \"status\": \"A\", \"isMainCard\": true },\n191\t      { \"cardNumber\": \"540000******8870\", \"cardNumberLast4\": \"8870\", \"productName\": \"Yıldız Tasarım\", \"status\": \"A\", \"isMainCard\": true }\n192\t    ],\n193\t    \"validCardsCount\": 4,\n194\t    \"exitState\": \"MULTI_CARD\"\n195\t  },\n196\t  \"result\": { \"returnCode\": \"1\", \"...\": \"\" }\n197\t}\n198\t```\n199\t\n200\t- `exitState`: `\"1_CARD\"` or `\"MULTI_CARD\"`.\n201\t- Only active cards come back (`status` `\"A\"`). `isMainCard` is `false` for a card that isn't the customer's own main card; it's still in the list.\n202\t- Two cards can have the same product name and the same last four digits (see above). Don't assume `cardNumberLast4` is unique.\n203\t- `productName` is free text from the card system, whatever was typed in there.\n204\t- Masking: in the **test environment** `cardNumber` comes back masked, first 6 and last 4 digits visible (`540000******1234`). What production returns in this field I still have to confirm with the card platform team; build so that it works with the masked form.\n205\t\n206\tErrors: 404 `FB_IVR_CHANNEL_102`, 500 `FBE00099`.\n207\t\n208\t### 5.2 `cards.balance`\n209\t\n210\tRequest:\n211\t\n212\t```json\n213\t{\n214\t  \"data\": { \"cardNumber\": \"540000******1234\" },\n215\t  \"call\": { \"ani\": \"<caller's number>\", \"lang\": \"tr\" }\n216\t}\n217\t```\n218\t\n219\tResponse:\n220\t\n221\t```json\n222\t{\n223\t  \"data\": {\n224\t    \"cardNumber\": \"5400********1234\",\n225\t    \"cardNumberLast4\": \"1234\",\n226\t    \"productName\": \"Yıldız Pratik Classic\",\n227\t    \"statementDate\": \"05.10.2026\",\n228\t    \"lastPayDate\": \"15.10.2026\",\n229\t    \"nextStatementDate\": \"05.11.2026\",\n230\t    \"nextLastPayDate\": \"15.11.2026\",\n231\t    \"debtTL\": {\n232\t      \"statementPaymentStatus\": \"HAS_DEBT\",\n233\t      \"transferAmount\": 0.0,\n234\t      \"statementDebt\": 5000.13,\n235\t      \"minPayment\": 2000.05,\n236\t      \"remainingDebt\": 5000.13,\n237\t      \"remainingMinPayment\": 2000.05,\n238\t      \"currentPeriodExpense\": 0.0,\n239\t      \"totalDebt\": 5000.13,\n240\t      \"totalDebtPaymentStatus\": \"HAS_DEBT\",\n241\t      \"remainAutomaticPaymentAmount\": 0.0\n242\t    },\n243\t    \"debtUSD\": {\n244\t      \"statementPaymentStatus\": \"NO_DEBT\",\n245\t      \"transferAmount\": 0.0,\n246\t      \"statementDebt\": 0.0,\n247\t      \"minPayment\": 0.0,\n248\t      \"remainingDebt\": 0.0,\n249\t      \"remainingMinPayment\": 0.0,\n250\t      \"currentPeriodExpense\": 0.0,\n251\t      \"totalDebt\": 0.0,\n252\t      \"totalDebtPaymentStatus\": \"NO_DEBT\",\n253\t      \"remainAutomaticPaymentAmount\": 0.0\n254\t    },\n255\t    \"debtEUR\": { \"...\": \"same fields as debtUSD\" },\n256\t    \"debtGBP\": { \"...\": \"same fields as debtUSD\" },\n257\t    \"statementPaymentStatus\": \"HAS_DEBT\",\n258\t    \"totalDebtPaymentStatus\": \"HAS_DEBT\",\n259\t    \"automaticPaymentFlag\": \"N\"\n260\t  },\n261\t  \"result\": { \"returnCode\": \"1\", \"...\": \"\" }\n262\t}\n263\t```\n264\t\n265\tField notes (my understanding from the card team, Selin please correct me if the business meaning is different):\n266\t\n267\t- `statementDate` / `lastPayDate`: the last issued statement and its due date. `nextStatementDate` / `nextLastPayDate`: the coming ones.\n268\t- One block per currency: `debtTL`, `debtUSD`, `debtEUR`, `debtGBP`. **A block can be missing** (some cards have no GBP block at all). Missing block is not the same as a block with `NO_DEBT`; don't invent zeros for it.\n269\t- In each block: `statementDebt` is the statement debt, `minPayment` the minimum payment on the statement; `remainingDebt` / `remainingMinPayment` what is left of those after payments made since; `totalDebt` includes spending after the statement; `currentPeriodExpense` that spending; `transferAmount` carried over from the previous period.\n270\t- `statementPaymentStatus` / `totalDebtPaymentStatus`: `\"HAS_DEBT\"` or `\"NO_DEBT\"`, per block and once more at the top level for the card overall.\n271\t- `totalDebt` can be negative (credit balance on that currency). Status is `NO_DEBT` then.\n272\t- `automaticPaymentFlag`: `\"Y\"` / `\"N\"`. `remainAutomaticPaymentAmount` is only meaningful when it's `\"Y\"`.\n273\t- Heads-up: the `cardNumber` in this answer is masked differently from the card list (first 4 + last 4, `5400********1234`). Don't use it to match against the list; use `cardNumberLast4` or just keep the number you sent.\n274\t\n275\tErrors: 400 `INVALID_ARGUMENTS_ERROR` (empty card number), 404 `FB_IVR_CHANNEL_120`, 500 `FBE00099`. Example 404:\n276\t\n277\t```json\n278\t{\n279\t  \"result\": {\n280\t    \"guid\": \"<trace id>\",\n281\t    \"returnCode\": \"FB_IVR_CHANNEL_120\",\n282\t    \"messageText\": \"Kart bakiye servisi hatası: kart bulunamadı\",\n283\t    \"code\": \"FB_IVR_CHANNEL_120\",\n284\t    \"message\": \"Kart bakiye servisi hatası: kart bulunamadı\",\n285\t    \"type\": \"WARNING\",\n286\t    \"details\": { \"guid\": \"<trace id>\", \"rootCause\": \"NotFoundException\", \"errorOrigin\": \"<gateway>\" }\n287\t  }\n288\t}\n289\t```\n290\t\n291\t(The message text here is just my example; only the code matters.)\n292\t\n293\t### 5.3 `statement.week.get`\n294\t\n295\tRequest:\n296\t\n297\t```json\n298\t{\n299\t  \"data\": { \"cardNumber\": \"540000******3201\" },\n300\t  \"call\": { \"ani\": \"<caller's number>\", \"lang\": \"tr\" }\n301\t}\n302\t```\n303\t\n304\tResponse:\n305\t\n306\t```json\n307\t{\n308\t  \"data\": {\n309\t    \"cardNumber\": \"540000******3201\",\n310\t    \"cardNumberLast4\": \"3201\",\n311\t    \"productName\": \"Yıldız Pratik\",\n312\t    \"statementWeek\": 3,\n313\t    \"statementDay\": 16,\n314\t    \"statementDate\": \"16.10.2026\",\n315\t    \"lastPayDate\": \"26.10.2026\",\n316\t    \"nextStatementDate\": \"16.11.2026\",\n317\t    \"nextLastPayDate\": \"26.11.2026\",\n318\t    \"relationType\": \"M\",\n319\t    \"relatedCardNumber\": \"540000******3202\",\n320\t    \"relatedCardProductName\": \"Yıldız Premium\"\n321\t  },\n322\t  \"result\": { \"returnCode\": \"1\", \"...\": \"\" }\n323\t}\n324\t```\n325\t\n326\t(Taken from test customer 92220795, who has two cards on one statement.)\n327\t\n328\t- `statementWeek`: 1 to 4, the week of the month the statement is cut. `statementDay`: day of the month. Both numbers here.\n329\t- `relationType`: empty string when the card has its own statement. `\"M\"` or `\"C\"` when two cards share one statement: `\"M\"` = this is the main card of the pair, `\"C\"` = this is the card linked to it. `relatedCardNumber` / `relatedCardProductName` then name the other card; otherwise they are empty strings. Changing the week on either card moves the shared statement, i.e. both cards.\n330\t- There is **no field telling you whether the customer can still change the week** (yearly limit etc.). Kerem said the rule lives in the card system and the change call either goes through or it doesn't; I'll ask whether we can expose a flag, no promises.\n331\t\n332\tErrors: 404 `FB_IVR_CHANNEL_121`, 500 `FBE00099`.\n333\t\n334\t### 5.4 `statement.week.decide`\n335\t\n336\tPreview only. Tells you which statement date, due date and day the card would get with the requested week. **It does not change the card.**\n337\t\n338\tRequest:\n339\t\n340\t```json\n341\t{\n342\t  \"data\": {\n343\t    \"cardNumber\": \"540000******1234\",\n344\t    \"newStatementWeek\": 2,\n345\t    \"statementDay\": 5\n346\t  },\n347\t  \"call\": { \"ani\": \"<caller's number>\", \"lang\": \"tr\" }\n348\t}\n349\t```\n350\t\n351\t- `newStatementWeek`: 1-4, number or string both fine.\n352\t- `statementDay`: the card's current `statementDay` from `statement.week.get`.\n353\t- Asking for the week the card already has: there is nothing to preview, you'll get an error, so don't call it for that.\n354\t\n355\tResponse:\n356\t\n357\t```json\n358\t{\n359\t  \"data\": {\n360\t    \"statementDate\": \"09.11.2026\",\n361\t    \"statementDueDate\": \"19.11.2026\",\n362\t    \"statementDay\": 9,\n363\t    \"responseCode\": 0,\n364\t    \"responseMessage\": \"Success\"\n365\t  },\n366\t  \"result\": { \"returnCode\": \"1\", \"...\": \"\" }\n367\t}\n368\t```\n369\t\n370\t`statementDate` / `statementDueDate` are the first statement and due date under the requested week. Note the due date is `statementDueDate` here, not `lastPayDate` like elsewhere (not my naming...). `responseCode` `0` = fine.\n371\t\n372\tErrors: 400 `INVALID_ARGUMENTS_ERROR`, 500 `FBE00099`.\n373\t\n374\t### 5.5 `statement.week.change`\n375\t\n376\tThis one changes the card. Call it only after the caller has agreed to the dates from 5.4.\n377\t\n378\tRequest:\n379\t\n380\t```json\n381\t{\n382\t  \"data\": {\n383\t    \"cardNumber\": \"540000******1234\",\n384\t    \"newStatementWeek\": 2,\n385\t    \"statementDay\": 5\n386\t  },\n387\t  \"call\": { \"ani\": \"<caller's number>\", \"lang\": \"tr\" }\n388\t}\n389\t```\n390\t\n391\t`statementDay` is required by the request model even though the backend recalculates the day. Send the current day.\n392\t\n393\tResponse:\n394\t\n395\t```json\n396\t{\n397\t  \"data\": {\n398\t    \"responseCode\": 0,\n399\t    \"responseMessage\": \"00\",\n400\t    \"statementWeek\": \"2\",\n401\t    \"statementDay\": \"9\",\n402\t    \"exitState\": \"OK\"\n403\t  },\n404\t  \"result\": { \"returnCode\": \"1\", \"...\": \"\" }\n405\t}\n406\t```\n407\t\n408\tCareful: `statementWeek` and `statementDay` come back as **strings** here (numbers everywhere else). Ignore `statementDay` in this answer; the dates to tell the caller are the ones from 5.4. Success = `exitState` `\"OK\"` and `responseCode` `0`.\n409\t\n410\tErrors: 400 `INVALID_ARGUMENTS_ERROR`, 401 (token), 500 `FBE00099`.\n411\t\n412\t### 5.6 `contact.email.get`\n413\t\n414\tRequest:\n415\t\n416\t```json\n417\t{\n418\t  \"data\": { \"customerNo\": 90000001, \"intAddressInfoType\": \"E\" },\n419\t  \"call\": { \"ani\": \"<caller's number>\", \"lang\": \"tr\" }\n420\t}\n421\t```\n422\t\n423\t`intAddressInfoType`: `\"E\"` e-mail, `\"P\"` phone, `\"B\"` both. Empty value gives a 400 (`\"intAddressInfoType cannot be empty\"`). The test environment returns the same answer whatever you ask for.\n424\t\n425\tResponse, e-mail registered:\n426\t\n427\t```json\n428\t{\n429\t  \"data\": {\n430\t    \"communicationType\": \"Both\",\n431\t    \"ibanCountryCode\": \"90\",\n432\t    \"ibanAreaCode\": \"500\",\n433\t    \"ibanPhoneNum\": \"0000000\",\n434\t    \"ibanEmail\": \"ornek@freyabank.example\",\n435\t    \"ttsEmail\": \"ornek@freyabank.example\",\n436\t    \"commGramType\": \"A\",\n437\t    \"exitState\": \"Done\"\n438\t  },\n439\t  \"result\": { \"returnCode\": \"1\", \"...\": \"\" }\n440\t}\n441\t```\n442\t\n443\tResponse, only a phone registered:\n444\t\n445\t```json\n446\t{\n447\t  \"data\": {\n448\t    \"communicationType\": \"Phone\",\n449\t    \"ibanCountryCode\": \"90\",\n450\t    \"ibanAreaCode\": \"500\",\n451\t    \"ibanPhoneNum\": \"0000000\",\n452\t    \"ibanEmail\": \"\",\n453\t    \"ttsEmail\": \"\",\n454\t    \"commGramType\": \"A\",\n455\t    \"exitState\": \"Done\"\n456\t  },\n457\t  \"result\": { \"returnCode\": \"1\", \"...\": \"\" }\n458\t}\n459\t```\n460\t\n461\t- The registered e-mail is `ibanEmail`. `ttsEmail` is the same address prepared for reading aloud (today identical in practice).\n462\t- No registered e-mail = `communicationType` `\"Phone\"` and/or empty `ibanEmail`. Check the field, not only the type.\n463\t- The `iban*` prefix is historical, it has nothing to do with IBANs.\n464\t\n465\tErrors: 400 `INVALID_ARGUMENTS_ERROR`, 500 `FBE00099`.\n466\t\n467\t### 5.7 `statement.pdf.send`\n468\t\n469\tSends the statement PDF to the address in the request.\n470\t\n471\tRequest:\n472\t\n473\t```json\n474\t{\n475\t  \"data\": {\n476\t    \"customerNo\": \"90000001\",\n477\t    \"cardNumber\": \"540000******1234\",\n478\t    \"currencyType\": \"TL\",\n479\t    \"statementDate\": \"05.10.2026\",\n480\t    \"emailAddress\": \"ornek@freyabank.example\"\n481\t  },\n482\t  \"call\": { \"ani\": \"<caller's number>\", \"lang\": \"tr\" }\n483\t}\n484\t```\n485\t\n486\t- `statementDate`: the `statementDate` from `cards.balance` (the issued statement).\n487\t- `currencyType`: `\"TL\"`, `\"USD\"`, `\"EUR\"` or `\"GBP\"`.\n488\t- `emailAddress`: the backend sends to whatever is in this field. It does **not** check it against the registered address. Whether any address other than the registered one is acceptable is a business/compliance question, Elçin and Selin's call, not mine.\n489\t\n490\tResponse:\n491\t\n492\t```json\n493\t{\n494\t  \"data\": { \"exitState\": \"DONE\" },\n495\t  \"result\": { \"returnCode\": \"1\", \"...\": \"\" }\n496\t}\n497\t```\n498\t\n499\tErrors: 400 `INVALID_ARGUMENTS_ERROR`, 404 `FB_IVR_CHANNEL_107` (no PDF for that statement), 500 `FBE00099`. Example 404:\n500\t\n501\t```json\n502\t{\n503\t  \"result\": {\n504\t    \"guid\": \"f0e1d2c3b4a5968778695a4b3c2d0466\",\n505\t    \"returnCode\": \"FB_IVR_CHANNEL_107\",\n506\t    \"messageText\": \"Pdf dokümanı bulunamadı\",\n507\t    \"code\": \"FB_IVR_CHANNEL_107\",\n508\t    \"message\": \"Pdf dokümanı bulunamadı\",\n509\t    \"type\": \"WARNING\",\n510\t    \"details\": {\n511\t      \"guid\": \"f0e1d2c3b4a5968778695a4b3c2d0466\",\n512\t      \"rootCause\": \"com.freyabank.ivrgateway.ivrchannel.rest.exception.NotFoundException\",\n513\t      \"errorOrigin\": \"ivr-gateway-test:ivr-channel-v0\"\n514\t    }\n515\t  }\n516\t}\n517\t```\n518\t\n519\t## 6. Test environment\n520\t\n521\t- Same paths and shapes as above. The synthetic customers and every answer they get are in `test-environment/test-customers.json` (overview in `test-environment/test-customers.md`). Nothing in there is a real person or card.\n522\t- If you build your own mock, it has to return those answers exactly (status code and body). For a customer or card that isn't in the file, return the 404 for that service from section 4 (for services without a 404 there, a 400 `INVALID_ARGUMENTS_ERROR` body).\n523\t- Only the `data` fields decide the answer in test. Token, headers and `call` are not checked.\n524\t\n525\t## 7. Open points (as of today)\n526\t\n527\t1. Client timeout and retry policy: not agreed. My suggestion is no automatic retry on `statement.week.change` and `statement.pdf.send` (both have side effects).\n528\t2. Card number masking in production `cards.list`: confirming with the card platform team.\n529\t3. A flag for \"can still change the statement week this year\": asked, no answer.\n530\t4. Rate limits per client: will share when the gateway team sets them up. Shouldn't matter at pilot volume.\n531\t\n532\tMert"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01S2HeanQ121N3iUrzXcmepZ","session_id":"adf52405-6942-4d44-9d85-c3233da23ca1","uuid":"c566ce33-ddc5-4a4d-b536-7f67bf787c98","timestamp":"2026-10-11T19:19:24.772Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Write rules.md from docs"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfvzP4xDESUW1qjgiD9jc","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01BDATBKfxPoELtrhUSY6Prs","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"C:\\Users\\Burak\\Desktop\\Projects\\agent-builder\\.claude\\worktrees\\final-case\\.builder\\agentic\\final8\\kit\\tools-and-code.md","offset":143,"limit":370},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":12281,"cache_read_input_tokens":96558,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":12281,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":16,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"adf52405-6942-4d44-9d85-c3233da23ca1","uuid":"0ebb8cb3-1410-479d-b8e4-5126f39f7d8b","timestamp":"2026-10-11T19:19:25.022Z","request_id":"req_011CfvzP2MBsapas75befbQA"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01BDATBKfxPoELtrhUSY6Prs","type":"tool_result","content":"143\t## `pre_actions` versus `tools`\n144\t\n145\tThe placement decides who fires the call.\n146\t\n147\t| Placement | Node type | Who calls it | When |\n148\t|---|---|---|---|\n149\t| `pre_actions` | `api_request`, `code` | The engine | On node entry, before any model turn |\n150\t| `tools` | `conversation` | The dialogue model | During a turn, when the model decides to |\n151\t| Workflow-level `tools` with `config.alwaysRunsAt` | none | The engine | At call start or call end |\n152\t\n153\tThe platform removes `tools` from non-conversation nodes and `pre_actions` from conversation nodes. It does so without an error: the JSON you sent looks fine, the create call succeeds, and the stored node has an empty array. A node cannot use both.\n154\t\n155\t**Use `pre_actions`** when the call must always run and the model has nothing to decide. Example: after the order number is known, always fetch the order.\n156\t\n157\t**Use `tools`** when the call depends on what the user says. Example: cancel the order only if the caller asks for it.\n158\t\n159\tDifferences in behaviour:\n160\t\n161\t| | `pre_actions` | `tools` |\n162\t|---|---|---|\n163\t| Can be skipped | No | Yes. The model may not call it if the description and the prompt do not push for it |\n164\t| Timing | Node entry | Inside a turn. The model can call a tool, speak, then call another |\n165\t| Result in the conversation | A tool call and tool result pair is added to the history | The same |\n166\t| `response_mapping` lives | On the **node** | On the **tool** |\n167\t| `description` | Ignored | The only text the model reads about the tool |\n168\t\n169\tTo run a fixed call before a tool-driven conversation, use a passthrough `api_request` node and transition from it into the conversation node. Do not build a long chain of passthrough nodes for optional actions. Put those into one conversation node with several tools.\n170\t\n171\t### A `pre_actions` node\n172\t\n173\t```json\n174\t{\n175\t  \"name\": \"fetch_order\",\n176\t  \"node_type\": \"api_request\",\n177\t  \"passthrough\": true,\n178\t  \"passthrough_default_transition\": \"present\",\n179\t  \"on_enter\": { \"type\": \"silent\" },\n180\t  \"pre_actions\": [\n181\t    {\n182\t      \"type\": \"function\",\n183\t      \"config\": { \"timeoutMs\": 10000, \"maxRetries\": 2 },\n184\t      \"function\": {\n185\t        \"name\": \"get_order\",\n186\t        \"handler\": \"__function__:api_call\",\n187\t        \"description\": \"Get the order.\",\n188\t        \"parameters\": {\n189\t          \"type\": \"object\",\n190\t          \"properties\": {\n191\t            \"url\": { \"type\": \"string\", \"default\": \"https://api.example.com/v1/get_order\" },\n192\t            \"method\": { \"type\": \"string\", \"default\": \"POST\" },\n193\t            \"headers\": { \"type\": \"object\", \"properties\": { \"Content-Type\": { \"type\": \"string\", \"default\": \"application/json\" } } },\n194\t            \"body\": { \"type\": \"object\", \"properties\": { \"order_number\": { \"type\": \"string\", \"default\": \"{{order_number}}\" } }, \"required\": [] }\n195\t          }\n196\t        }\n197\t      }\n198\t    }\n199\t  ],\n200\t  \"response_mapping\": [\n201\t    { \"path\": \"data.items\", \"variable\": \"items\" }\n202\t  ],\n203\t  \"transitions\": [\n204\t    { \"target_node\": \"present\", \"description\": \"always\", \"condition\": { \"operator\": \"always\" } }\n205\t  ]\n206\t}\n207\t```\n208\t\n209\tThe last `always` transition is mandatory. A passthrough node with no matching transition deadlocks.\n210\t\n211\t### Tool descriptions\n212\t\n213\tIn `tools`, the `description` decides whether the model calls the tool.\n214\t\n215\t- Write it as an instruction with the trigger: \"CALL it every time the caller gives an order number.\"\n216\t- A plain description such as \"Looks up an order\" lets the model acknowledge the request in words and never call the tool. That is the most common failure of nodes with many tools.\n217\t- If two tools could match, say which one not to use and name the other.\n218\t- In `task_message`, put the tool rules before long reference text and state: when a rule says to use a tool, call the tool.\n219\t- The working builder also tells the model to call one tool per message and to speak after every tool result. Verified in a working builder. In a trial call a model turn with no text and no tool call produced the error event \"The model returned no speakable content\".\n220\t\n221\tA tool is available only in the nodes that list it. Seen in a trial call: the model called a tool that the current node did not list. The result was the text \"The function `<name>` is not currently available.\"\n222\t\n223\t## `response_mapping`\n224\t\n225\t`response_mapping` copies values from a call result into named variables. It needs no model call.\n226\t\n227\t```json\n228\t{\n229\t  \"response_mapping\": [\n230\t    { \"path\": \"data.status\", \"variable\": \"order_status\" },\n231\t    { \"path\": \"data.items\", \"variable\": \"items\" }\n232\t  ]\n233\t}\n234\t```\n235\t\n236\t| Field | Meaning |\n237\t|---|---|\n238\t| `path` | Dot path into the tool result |\n239\t| `variable` | Name of the variable to set |\n240\t\n241\t### Placement\n242\t\n243\t- For a tool in `tools`: inside the tool object, next to `function` and `config`.\n244\t- For `pre_actions`: at the **node level**. A `response_mapping` nested inside a `pre_actions` entry is ignored and the variable never appears.\n245\t\n246\t`extraction_schema` follows the same placement rule if you use it on a call result.\n247\t\n248\t### Where a result lands (trial findings)\n249\t\n250\tSeen in a trial call: the result of an `api_call` is an envelope around the HTTP response.\n251\t\n252\t```json\n253\t{\n254\t  \"status\": \"success\",\n255\t  \"data\": { \"ok\": true, \"verified\": true, \"attempts_left\": 3 },\n256\t  \"message\": \"Successfully executed POST request\",\n257\t  \"status_code\": 200,\n258\t  \"header\": { \"Content-Type\": \"application/json\" }\n259\t}\n260\t```\n261\t\n262\t`data` is the JSON body the API returned. `header` holds the HTTP response headers.\n263\t\n264\tThe engine also writes every result into the variable table by itself, flattened with dots. You do not need a mapping for these. For a tool named `check_answer` whose API returned `{\"ok\": true, \"verified\": true, \"attempts_left\": 3}`, the trial call's variable table held:\n265\t\n266\t| Variable | Value |\n267\t|---|---|\n268\t| `check_answer_args.<field>` | Each argument the model sent |\n269\t| `check_answer_response.status` | `success` |\n270\t| `check_answer_response.data.ok` | `true` (a string) |\n271\t| `check_answer_response.data.verified` | `true` (a string) |\n272\t| `check_answer_response.data.attempts_left` | `3` |\n273\t| `check_answer_response.message` | `Successfully executed POST request` |\n274\t| `check_answer_response.status_code` | `200` |\n275\t| `check_answer_response.header.<Header-Name>` | One variable per response header |\n276\t| `check_answer_status_code` | `200` |\n277\t| `check_answer_success_count` | Number of successful calls so far |\n278\t\n279\tEach call of the same tool overwrites the previous `_args` and `_response` values.\n280\t\n281\t### Which mapping paths work\n282\t\n283\t- **Start the path at the envelope, with `data.`**. If the API returns `{\"ok\": true}`, map `data.ok`. If it returns `{\"products\": [...]}`, map `data.products`. Verified in a working builder, on a tool-level mapping and on a node-level mapping.\n284\t- A path without the `data.` prefix (for the same body: `ok`) is not confirmed. A smaller example builder uses it. In an early trial call the mapped variables stayed unset and the transitions on them never fired. The mapping paths of that call are not recorded. The builder that passed uses `data.` paths.\n285\t- The flattened key can be used directly as a transition variable: `check_answer_response.data.verified`. Verified in a working builder, which tests the mapped variable and the flattened key in the same OR group as a safeguard.\n286\t- Booleans arrive as strings. The trial call showed `true` in lower case in the flattened keys. Test both `\"true\"` and `\"True\"`, or map a string field.\n287\t- A mapped list or object: the exact form it has later inside `vars` is not confirmed. The working builder reads it defensively. It accepts a list, a JSON string, and flattened keys.\n288\t\n289\t## Routing on tool results\n290\t\n291\tRouting runs again after a tool result arrives. A transition on a result variable fires in the same turn, before any further model turn. Seen in a trial call.\n292\t\n293\t```json\n294\t{\n295\t  \"transitions\": [\n296\t    {\n297\t      \"target_node\": \"load_order\",\n298\t      \"description\": \"The check passed\",\n299\t      \"condition\": {\n300\t        \"logic\": \"or\",\n301\t        \"conditions\": [\n302\t          { \"variable\": \"check_passed\", \"operator\": \"equals\", \"operand\": \"true\" },\n303\t          { \"variable\": \"check_passed\", \"operator\": \"equals\", \"operand\": \"True\" },\n304\t          { \"variable\": \"check_answer_response.data.verified\", \"operator\": \"equals\", \"operand\": \"true\" },\n305\t          { \"variable\": \"check_answer_response.data.verified\", \"operator\": \"equals\", \"operand\": \"True\" }\n306\t        ]\n307\t      }\n308\t    }\n309\t  ]\n310\t}\n311\t```\n312\t\n313\tRules:\n314\t\n315\t- On a passthrough node, end with an `always` transition so that a failed or empty response does not deadlock.\n316\t- Use a tool result to leave a conversation node. The pattern \"a closing tool sets a variable, a transition on that variable leads to an `end_call` node\" is verified in a working builder.\n317\t- To leave a node that carries out an action, do not re-test the user's earlier intent with `matches_intent`. The router looks at the latest user message and can route away before the tool was called. Extract a follow-up enum with a default such as `stay`, and branch with `equals`.\n318\t\n319\t### Success and failure counters\n320\t\n321\tThe engine keeps two integer variables per call target. No mapping is needed.\n322\t\n323\t| Placement | Variables |\n324\t|---|---|\n325\t| `pre_actions` node | `<nodeId>_success_count`, `<nodeId>_failure_count` |\n326\t| Tool in `tools` with handler `api_call` | `<toolName>_success_count`, `<toolName>_failure_count` |\n327\t\n328\tSeen in a trial call: `check_answer_success_count` was `2` after two successful calls, and a code node named `setup` produced `setup_success_count` with the value `1`.\n329\t\n330\t```json\n331\t{\n332\t  \"transitions\": [\n333\t    { \"target_node\": \"give_up\", \"description\": \"The lookup failed three times\", \"condition\": { \"logic\": \"or\", \"conditions\": [ { \"variable\": \"lookup_order_failure_count\", \"operator\": \"greater_than\", \"operand\": 2 } ] } },\n334\t    { \"target_node\": \"ask_again\", \"description\": \"always\", \"condition\": { \"operator\": \"always\" } }\n335\t  ]\n336\t}\n337\t```\n338\t\n339\t### `suppress_tool_results`\n340\t\n341\tA node-level boolean for nodes that run API calls. With `true` the dialogue model sees only a short `{status: \"success\"}` summary in place of the raw response. Variables are still mapped and routing still sees them. Use it for large responses, or when the mapped variables already hold everything the model needs.\n342\t\n343\t### Background tools\n344\t\n345\tA tool with `\"config\": {\"interruptible\": false}` runs in the background. A user interruption does not cancel it. Its result arrives one or more turns later. The mapped variables, the `_response` keys and the counters are filled as usual, but not in the same turn. A transition that reads them right after the call can find them unset. Gate on the variable with `is_set`.\n346\t\n347\t## Retries and timeouts\n348\t\n349\t| Setting | Where | Default | What it does |\n350\t|---|---|---|---|\n351\t| `timeoutMs` | `config` | not documented | Time limit of one attempt, in milliseconds |\n352\t| `maxRetries` | `config` | not documented | Tool-level retries after a failed response |\n353\t| `connect_retries` | `parameters.properties`, as a baked parameter | `2` | Retries when the connection could not be established |\n354\t| `timeout_ms` | `code_exec` parameter | `30000` | Time limit of a code snippet |\n355\t\n356\t`connect_retries` in detail:\n357\t\n358\t- It retries only failures to establish the connection: connection refused, or reset during the TCP or TLS handshake. The request never reached the server, so a repeat is safe even for POST.\n359\t- HTTP error statuses and timeouts are not retried by it.\n360\t- Hard cap `5`. `0` means a single attempt.\n361\t- Back-off is 150 ms times the attempt number, with a new connection each time.\n362\t- All attempts share one 30 second budget for the request.\n363\t- Declare it next to `url` and `method`: `\"connect_retries\": {\"type\": \"number\", \"default\": 0}`. It is not a `config` field.\n364\t\n365\tThe working builder sets `maxRetries` to `0` on tools that write data and to `2` on its read-only `pre_actions` call. A retried write can record the same thing twice if the API is not idempotent.\n366\t\n367\t## Pre and post Python hooks\n368\t\n369\tAn `api_call` tool can carry two Python snippets. They run on the server, in a sandbox, right before and right after the call. They are top-level fields of the tool object.\n370\t\n371\t```json\n372\t{\n373\t  \"type\": \"function\",\n374\t  \"config\": { \"timeoutMs\": 10000, \"maxRetries\": 0 },\n375\t  \"function\": {\n376\t    \"name\": \"check_answer\",\n377\t    \"handler\": \"__function__:api_call\",\n378\t    \"description\": \"Check one answer. CALL it every time the caller answers the question.\",\n379\t    \"parameters\": {\n380\t      \"type\": \"object\",\n381\t      \"properties\": {\n382\t        \"url\": { \"type\": \"string\", \"default\": \"https://api.example.com/v1/check_answer\" },\n383\t        \"method\": { \"type\": \"string\", \"default\": \"POST\" },\n384\t        \"body\": { \"type\": \"object\", \"properties\": { \"answer\": { \"type\": \"string\", \"description\": \"The caller's answer\", \"default\": \"{{answer}}\" } }, \"required\": [\"answer\"] }\n385\t      }\n386\t    }\n387\t  },\n388\t  \"pre_process_code\": \"vars['member_code'] = str(vars.get('member_code') or '').strip().upper()\",\n389\t  \"post_process_code\": \"try:\\n    if isinstance(result, dict):\\n        result.pop('header', None)\\n        d = result.get('data')\\n        if isinstance(d, dict):\\n            d['what_to_do_next'] = 'Ask the next question.' if d.get('verified') else 'Ask the same question again.'\\nexcept Exception:\\n    pass\\n\"\n390\t}\n391\t```\n392\t\n393\t| Hook | Names available | Use |\n394\t|---|---|---|\n395\t| `pre_process_code` | `vars`: mutable dict that fills the `{{var}}` placeholders of the request. `config`: the substituted request with `url`, `method`, `headers`, `body` | Clean a value, or rewrite the whole request |\n396\t| `post_process_code` | `result`: the tool result, mutable | Reshape, shorten or extend the result |\n397\t\n398\tDocumented behaviour:\n399\t\n400\t- The post-hook runs before `response_mapping` and extraction see the result.\n401\t- An empty or whitespace-only string means no hook.\n402\t- Hooks are honoured only on `api_call` tools (and on the integration and knowledge search handlers). The platform removes them from any other handler when the workflow is saved.\n403\t- In a pre-hook, setting `config['body']` to a string sends that string as the raw body, with no JSON encoding. Set the `Content-Type` header yourself in that case.\n404\t\n405\t### What `result` looks like in a post-hook\n406\t\n407\tThe internal reference shows `result` as the response body. A working builder shows otherwise. Verified in a working builder:\n408\t\n409\t- For an `api_call`, `result` is the envelope described above: a dict with `status`, `data`, `message`, `status_code` and `header`. The API's JSON body is `result[\"data\"]`.\n410\t- A key that the hook adds inside `result[\"data\"]` reaches the dialogue model as part of the tool result. The working builder adds a `what_to_do_next` sentence there, and its prompt tells the model to obey it.\n411\t- `result.pop(\"header\", None)` removes the response headers. The working builder does this on every tool, so that headers do not take up space in the model's context.\n412\t- Mapping paths still start with `data.` when a post-hook is present.\n413\t\n414\tThe working builder writes every hook inside `try` and `except Exception: pass` and checks types with `isinstance` before use. What the platform does when a hook raises is not documented.\n415\t\n416\tSeen in a test on 10 October: a post-hook can read call variables and write new variables, and a later pre-hook reads them. Not confirmed: whether a change to `result` also changes the flattened `<tool>_response.*` variables.\n417\t\n418\t## Code nodes and `code_exec`\n419\t\n420\tA `code` node runs a Python snippet when it is entered. The snippet is a `code_exec` function in `pre_actions`.\n421\t\n422\t```json\n423\t{\n424\t  \"name\": \"compute_delivery\",\n425\t  \"node_type\": \"code\",\n426\t  \"passthrough\": true,\n427\t  \"passthrough_default_transition\": \"tell_delivery\",\n428\t  \"on_enter\": { \"type\": \"silent\" },\n429\t  \"pre_actions\": [\n430\t    {\n431\t      \"type\": \"function\",\n432\t      \"function\": {\n433\t        \"name\": \"code_exec\",\n434\t        \"handler\": \"__function__:code_exec\",\n435\t        \"parameters\": {\n436\t          \"type\": \"object\",\n437\t          \"properties\": {\n438\t            \"code\": { \"type\": \"string\", \"default\": \"import datetime\\ntry:\\n    start = datetime.date.fromisoformat(str(vars.get('shipped_on'))[:10])\\n    eta = start + datetime.timedelta(days=3)\\n    vars['delivery_day'] = eta.isoformat()\\n    vars['delivery_state'] = 'known'\\nexcept Exception:\\n    vars['delivery_day'] = ''\\n    vars['delivery_state'] = 'unknown'\\n\" },\n439\t            \"timeout_ms\": { \"type\": \"number\", \"default\": 10000 }\n440\t          }\n441\t        }\n442\t      }\n443\t    }\n444\t  ],\n445\t  \"transitions\": [\n446\t    { \"target_node\": \"tell_delivery\", \"description\": \"always\", \"condition\": { \"operator\": \"always\" } }\n447\t  ]\n448\t}\n449\t```\n450\t\n451\t| Parameter | Meaning |\n452\t|---|---|\n453\t| `code` | The Python source, as a baked `default` string |\n454\t| `timeout_ms` | Time limit in milliseconds. Default `30000` |\n455\t\n456\t### Reading and writing variables\n457\t\n458\t- The snippet gets a dict named `vars`. It holds the current workflow variables.\n459\t- Read with `vars.get(\"name\")`. A variable that was never set is absent.\n460\t- Write with `vars[\"name\"] = value`. Every key the snippet assigns becomes a workflow variable.\n461\t\n462\tVerified in a working builder:\n463\t\n464\t- Call variables are in `vars`. The builder reads `vars.get(\"now\")`, a call variable passed with the simulation.\n465\t- The result of an earlier `api_request` node is reachable from `vars` in a later code node. The builder looks for it under the mapped variable name first and under the flattened result keys second. Which of the two held the data is not recorded.\n466\t- A string written to `vars[\"date_facts\"]` in a code node resolves as `{{date_facts}}` in the `task_message` of later conversation nodes. Multi-line strings work.\n467\t- The standard modules `json`, `re` and `datetime` can be imported.\n468\t- The same snippet can run in two code nodes at different points of the call. The later run sees the variables added in between.\n469\t\n470\t### What a code node returns\n471\t\n472\tSeen in a trial call: the result of a successful `code_exec` is\n473\t\n474\t```json\n475\t{ \"status\": \"success\", \"data\": null, \"message\": \"Code executed successfully\", \"status_code\": 200 }\n476\t```\n477\t\n478\tThe snippet's output is not in `data`. Its values reach the workflow only through `vars`. After a code node named `setup` ran, the variable table held:\n479\t\n480\t| Variable | Value |\n481\t|---|---|\n482\t| each key the snippet wrote, for example `date_facts` | the value, with source `tool_result` and source node `setup` |\n483\t| `setup_response.status` | `success` |\n484\t| `setup_response.data` | `null` |\n485\t| `setup_response.message` | `Code executed successfully` |\n486\t| `setup_response.status_code` | `200` |\n487\t| `setup_status_code` | `200` |\n488\t| `setup_success_count` | `1` |\n489\t\n490\tFor a code node the automatic keys are named after the **node**, not after `code_exec`.\n491\t\n492\t### Rules for snippets\n493\t\n494\t- **Do not write `{{` or `}}` in the code.** The internal sources disagree on whether the `code` field is templated. One says it is exempt. Another says placeholders are resolved when the code runs and an unset one is left as literal text, which breaks the Python. The working builder removes every `{{` and `}}` from generated code (a nested dict literal such as `{'a': {'b': 1}}` ends in `}}`) and reads all values from `vars`.\n495\t- **Never let a snippet raise.** Wrap the body in `try` and `except`, and write a fallback value for every variable that a later prompt or condition reads. A prompt that references an unset variable shows the literal placeholder to the model.\n496\t- **Write strings for routing flags**, such as `known` and `unknown`. Do not route on a Python boolean. See \"Do not route on a boolean\" in workflow-format.md.\n497\t- Keep snippets fast. They block the conversation.\n498\t- Do arithmetic, date calculation and counting in code, and give the model the finished facts as text. Verified in a working builder: it computes a block of date facts in a code node and places it in the prompts.\n499\t- Treat a code node like an API node: `passthrough: true`, a `passthrough_default_transition`, and a last `always` transition.\n500\t\n501\t### Sandbox limits\n502\t\n503\t| Limit | Value |\n504\t|---|---|\n505\t| Time | `timeout_ms`, default `30000` |\n506\t| Modules | `json`, `re` and `datetime` are verified in a working builder. The full list is not documented |\n507\t| Network and file access | Not documented |\n508\t| Memory and output size | Not documented |\n509\t\n510\t### `code_exec` as a tool\n511\t\n512\t`code_exec` can also be a tool in the `tools` array of a conversation node, called by the model. Its parameters are top-level arguments, declared without `default` so the model fills them. For such a tool the `code` field resolves `{{var}}` when the tool is called, against the current variable table. The working builder does not use this form, so nothing about it is verified here."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"adf52405-6942-4d44-9d85-c3233da23ca1","uuid":"71732ac3-5e88-4bb9-a638-866f7dcbaa7d","timestamp":"2026-10-11T19:19:25.029Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\Users\\Burak\\Desktop\\Projects\\agent-builder\\.claude\\worktrees\\final-case\\.builder\\agentic\\final8\\kit\\tools-and-code.md","content":"## `pre_actions` versus `tools`\n\nThe placement decides who fires the call.\n\n| Placement | Node type | Who calls it | When |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `pre_actions` | `api_request`, `code` | The engine | On node entry, before any model turn |\n| `tools` | `conversation` | The dialogue model | During a turn, when the model decides to |\n| Workflow-level `tools` with `config.alwaysRunsAt` | none | The engine | At call start or call end |\n\nThe platform removes `tools` from non-conversation nodes and `pre_actions` from conversation nodes. It does so without an error: the JSON you sent looks fine, the create call succeeds, and the stored node has an empty array. A node cannot use both.\n\n**Use `pre_actions`** when the call must always run and the model has nothing to decide. Example: after the order number is known, always fetch the order.\n\n**Use `tools`** when the call depends on what the user says. Example: cancel the order only if the caller asks for it.\n\nDifferences in behaviour:\n\n| | `pre_actions` | `tools` |\n|---|---|---|\n| Can be skipped | No | Yes. The model may not call it if the description and the prompt do not push for it |\n| Timing | Node entry | Inside a turn. The model can call a tool, speak, then call another |\n| Result in the conversation | A tool call and tool result pair is added to the history | The same |\n| `response_mapping` lives | On the **node** | On the **tool** |\n| `description` | Ignored | The only text the model reads about the tool |\n\nTo run a fixed call before a tool-driven conversation, use a passthrough `api_request` node and transition from it into the conversation node. Do not build a long chain of passthrough nodes for optional actions. Put those into one conversation node with several tools.\n\n### A `pre_actions` node\n\n```json\n{\n  \"name\": \"fetch_order\",\n  \"node_type\": \"api_request\",\n  \"passthrough\": true,\n  \"passthrough_default_transition\": \"present\",\n  \"on_enter\": { \"type\": \"silent\" },\n  \"pre_actions\": [\n    {\n      \"type\": \"function\",\n      \"config\": { \"timeoutMs\": 10000, \"maxRetries\": 2 },\n      \"function\": {\n        \"name\": \"get_order\",\n        \"handler\": \"__function__:api_call\",\n        \"description\": \"Get the order.\",\n        \"parameters\": {\n          \"type\": \"object\",\n          \"properties\": {\n            \"url\": { \"type\": \"string\", \"default\": \"https://api.example.com/v1/get_order\" },\n            \"method\": { \"type\": \"string\", \"default\": \"POST\" },\n            \"headers\": { \"type\": \"object\", \"properties\": { \"Content-Type\": { \"type\": \"string\", \"default\": \"application/json\" } } },\n            \"body\": { \"type\": \"object\", \"properties\": { \"order_number\": { \"type\": \"string\", \"default\": \"{{order_number}}\" } }, \"required\": [] }\n          }\n        }\n      }\n    }\n  ],\n  \"response_mapping\": [\n    { \"path\": \"data.items\", \"variable\": \"items\" }\n  ],\n  \"transitions\": [\n    { \"target_node\": \"present\", \"description\": \"always\", \"condition\": { \"operator\": \"always\" } }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nThe last `always` transition is mandatory. A passthrough node with no matching transition deadlocks.\n\n### Tool descriptions\n\nIn `tools`, the `description` decides whether the model calls the tool.\n\n- Write it as an instruction with the trigger: \"CALL it every time the caller gives an order number.\"\n- A plain description such as \"Looks up an order\" lets the model acknowledge the request in words and never call the tool. That is the most common failure of nodes with many tools.\n- If two tools could match, say which one not to use and name the other.\n- In `task_message`, put the tool rules before long reference text and state: when a rule says to use a tool, call the tool.\n- The working builder also tells the model to call one tool per message and to speak after every tool result. Verified in a working builder. In a trial call a model turn with no text and no tool call produced the error event \"The model returned no speakable content\".\n\nA tool is available only in the nodes that list it. Seen in a trial call: the model called a tool that the current node did not list. The result was the text \"The function `<name>` is not currently available.\"\n\n## `response_mapping`\n\n`response_mapping` copies values from a call result into named variables. It needs no model call.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"response_mapping\": [\n    { \"path\": \"data.status\", \"variable\": \"order_status\" },\n    { \"path\": \"data.items\", \"variable\": \"items\" }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n| Field | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| `path` | Dot path into the tool result |\n| `variable` | Name of the variable to set |\n\n### Placement\n\n- For a tool in `tools`: inside the tool object, next to `function` and `config`.\n- For `pre_actions`: at the **node level**. A `response_mapping` nested inside a `pre_actions` entry is ignored and the variable never appears.\n\n`extraction_schema` follows the same placement rule if you use it on a call result.\n\n### Where a result lands (trial findings)\n\nSeen in a trial call: the result of an `api_call` is an envelope around the HTTP response.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"status\": \"success\",\n  \"data\": { \"ok\": true, \"verified\": true, \"attempts_left\": 3 },\n  \"message\": \"Successfully executed POST request\",\n  \"status_code\": 200,\n  \"header\": { \"Content-Type\": \"application/json\" }\n}\n```\n\n`data` is the JSON body the API returned. `header` holds the HTTP response headers.\n\nThe engine also writes every result into the variable table by itself, flattened with dots. You do not need a mapping for these. For a tool named `check_answer` whose API returned `{\"ok\": true, \"verified\": true, \"attempts_left\": 3}`, the trial call's variable table held:\n\n| Variable | Value |\n|---|---|\n| `check_answer_args.<field>` | Each argument the model sent |\n| `check_answer_response.status` | `success` |\n| `check_answer_response.data.ok` | `true` (a string) |\n| `check_answer_response.data.verified` | `true` (a string) |\n| `check_answer_response.data.attempts_left` | `3` |\n| `check_answer_response.message` | `Successfully executed POST request` |\n| `check_answer_response.status_code` | `200` |\n| `check_answer_response.header.<Header-Name>` | One variable per response header |\n| `check_answer_status_code` | `200` |\n| `check_answer_success_count` | Number of successful calls so far |\n\nEach call of the same tool overwrites the previous `_args` and `_response` values.\n\n### Which mapping paths work\n\n- **Start the path at the envelope, with `data.`**. If the API returns `{\"ok\": true}`, map `data.ok`. If it returns `{\"products\": [...]}`, map `data.products`. Verified in a working builder, on a tool-level mapping and on a node-level mapping.\n- A path without the `data.` prefix (for the same body: `ok`) is not confirmed. A smaller example builder uses it. In an early trial call the mapped variables stayed unset and the transitions on them never fired. The mapping paths of that call are not recorded. The builder that passed uses `data.` paths.\n- The flattened key can be used directly as a transition variable: `check_answer_response.data.verified`. Verified in a working builder, which tests the mapped variable and the flattened key in the same OR group as a safeguard.\n- Booleans arrive as strings. The trial call showed `true` in lower case in the flattened keys. Test both `\"true\"` and `\"True\"`, or map a string field.\n- A mapped list or object: the exact form it has later inside `vars` is not confirmed. The working builder reads it defensively. It accepts a list, a JSON string, and flattened keys.\n\n## Routing on tool results\n\nRouting runs again after a tool result arrives. A transition on a result variable fires in the same turn, before any further model turn. Seen in a trial call.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"transitions\": [\n    {\n      \"target_node\": \"load_order\",\n      \"description\": \"The check passed\",\n      \"condition\": {\n        \"logic\": \"or\",\n        \"conditions\": [\n          { \"variable\": \"check_passed\", \"operator\": \"equals\", \"operand\": \"true\" },\n          { \"variable\": \"check_passed\", \"operator\": \"equals\", \"operand\": \"True\" },\n          { \"variable\": \"check_answer_response.data.verified\", \"operator\": \"equals\", \"operand\": \"true\" },\n          { \"variable\": \"check_answer_response.data.verified\", \"operator\": \"equals\", \"operand\": \"True\" }\n        ]\n      }\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nRules:\n\n- On a passthrough node, end with an `always` transition so that a failed or empty response does not deadlock.\n- Use a tool result to leave a conversation node. The pattern \"a closing tool sets a variable, a transition on that variable leads to an `end_call` node\" is verified in a working builder.\n- To leave a node that carries out an action, do not re-test the user's earlier intent with `matches_intent`. The router looks at the latest user message and can route away before the tool was called. Extract a follow-up enum with a default such as `stay`, and branch with `equals`.\n\n### Success and failure counters\n\nThe engine keeps two integer variables per call target. No mapping is needed.\n\n| Placement | Variables |\n|---|---|\n| `pre_actions` node | `<nodeId>_success_count`, `<nodeId>_failure_count` |\n| Tool in `tools` with handler `api_call` | `<toolName>_success_count`, `<toolName>_failure_count` |\n\nSeen in a trial call: `check_answer_success_count` was `2` after two successful calls, and a code node named `setup` produced `setup_success_count` with the value `1`.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"transitions\": [\n    { \"target_node\": \"give_up\", \"description\": \"The lookup failed three times\", \"condition\": { \"logic\": \"or\", \"conditions\": [ { \"variable\": \"lookup_order_failure_count\", \"operator\": \"greater_than\", \"operand\": 2 } ] } },\n    { \"target_node\": \"ask_again\", \"description\": \"always\", \"condition\": { \"operator\": \"always\" } }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n### `suppress_tool_results`\n\nA node-level boolean for nodes that run API calls. With `true` the dialogue model sees only a short `{status: \"success\"}` summary in place of the raw response. Variables are still mapped and routing still sees them. Use it for large responses, or when the mapped variables already hold everything the model needs.\n\n### Background tools\n\nA tool with `\"config\": {\"interruptible\": false}` runs in the background. A user interruption does not cancel it. Its result arrives one or more turns later. The mapped variables, the `_response` keys and the counters are filled as usual, but not in the same turn. A transition that reads them right after the call can find them unset. Gate on the variable with `is_set`.\n\n## Retries and timeouts\n\n| Setting | Where | Default | What it does |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `timeoutMs` | `config` | not documented | Time limit of one attempt, in milliseconds |\n| `maxRetries` | `config` | not documented | Tool-level retries after a failed response |\n| `connect_retries` | `parameters.properties`, as a baked parameter | `2` | Retries when the connection could not be established |\n| `timeout_ms` | `code_exec` parameter | `30000` | Time limit of a code snippet |\n\n`connect_retries` in detail:\n\n- It retries only failures to establish the connection: connection refused, or reset during the TCP or TLS handshake. The request never reached the server, so a repeat is safe even for POST.\n- HTTP error statuses and timeouts are not retried by it.\n- Hard cap `5`. `0` means a single attempt.\n- Back-off is 150 ms times the attempt number, with a new connection each time.\n- All attempts share one 30 second budget for the request.\n- Declare it next to `url` and `method`: `\"connect_retries\": {\"type\": \"number\", \"default\": 0}`. It is not a `config` field.\n\nThe working builder sets `maxRetries` to `0` on tools that write data and to `2` on its read-only `pre_actions` call. A retried write can record the same thing twice if the API is not idempotent.\n\n## Pre and post Python hooks\n\nAn `api_call` tool can carry two Python snippets. They run on the server, in a sandbox, right before and right after the call. They are top-level fields of the tool object.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"type\": \"function\",\n  \"config\": { \"timeoutMs\": 10000, \"maxRetries\": 0 },\n  \"function\": {\n    \"name\": \"check_answer\",\n    \"handler\": \"__function__:api_call\",\n    \"description\": \"Check one answer. CALL it every time the caller answers the question.\",\n    \"parameters\": {\n      \"type\": \"object\",\n      \"properties\": {\n        \"url\": { \"type\": \"string\", \"default\": \"https://api.example.com/v1/check_answer\" },\n        \"method\": { \"type\": \"string\", \"default\": \"POST\" },\n        \"body\": { \"type\": \"object\", \"properties\": { \"answer\": { \"type\": \"string\", \"description\": \"The caller's answer\", \"default\": \"{{answer}}\" } }, \"required\": [\"answer\"] }\n      }\n    }\n  },\n  \"pre_process_code\": \"vars['member_code'] = str(vars.get('member_code') or '').strip().upper()\",\n  \"post_process_code\": \"try:\\n    if isinstance(result, dict):\\n        result.pop('header', None)\\n        d = result.get('data')\\n        if isinstance(d, dict):\\n            d['what_to_do_next'] = 'Ask the next question.' if d.get('verified') else 'Ask the same question again.'\\nexcept Exception:\\n    pass\\n\"\n}\n```\n\n| Hook | Names available | Use |\n|---|---|---|\n| `pre_process_code` | `vars`: mutable dict that fills the `{{var}}` placeholders of the request. `config`: the substituted request with `url`, `method`, `headers`, `body` | Clean a value, or rewrite the whole request |\n| `post_process_code` | `result`: the tool result, mutable | Reshape, shorten or extend the result |\n\nDocumented behaviour:\n\n- The post-hook runs before `response_mapping` and extraction see the result.\n- An empty or whitespace-only string means no hook.\n- Hooks are honoured only on `api_call` tools (and on the integration and knowledge search handlers). The platform removes them from any other handler when the workflow is saved.\n- In a pre-hook, setting `config['body']` to a string sends that string as the raw body, with no JSON encoding. Set the `Content-Type` header yourself in that case.\n\n### What `result` looks like in a post-hook\n\nThe internal reference shows `result` as the response body. A working builder shows otherwise. Verified in a working builder:\n\n- For an `api_call`, `result` is the envelope described above: a dict with `status`, `data`, `message`, `status_code` and `header`. The API's JSON body is `result[\"data\"]`.\n- A key that the hook adds inside `result[\"data\"]` reaches the dialogue model as part of the tool result. The working builder adds a `what_to_do_next` sentence there, and its prompt tells the model to obey it.\n- `result.pop(\"header\", None)` removes the response headers. The working builder does this on every tool, so that headers do not take up space in the model's context.\n- Mapping paths still start with `data.` when a post-hook is present.\n\nThe working builder writes every hook inside `try` and `except Exception: pass` and checks types with `isinstance` before use. What the platform does when a hook raises is not documented.\n\nSeen in a test on 10 October: a post-hook can read call variables and write new variables, and a later pre-hook reads them. Not confirmed: whether a change to `result` also changes the flattened `<tool>_response.*` variables.\n\n## Code nodes and `code_exec`\n\nA `code` node runs a Python snippet when it is entered. The snippet is a `code_exec` function in `pre_actions`.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"name\": \"compute_delivery\",\n  \"node_type\": \"code\",\n  \"passthrough\": true,\n  \"passthrough_default_transition\": \"tell_delivery\",\n  \"on_enter\": { \"type\": \"silent\" },\n  \"pre_actions\": [\n    {\n      \"type\": \"function\",\n      \"function\": {\n        \"name\": \"code_exec\",\n        \"handler\": \"__function__:code_exec\",\n        \"parameters\": {\n          \"type\": \"object\",\n          \"properties\": {\n            \"code\": { \"type\": \"string\", \"default\": \"import datetime\\ntry:\\n    start = datetime.date.fromisoformat(str(vars.get('shipped_on'))[:10])\\n    eta = start + datetime.timedelta(days=3)\\n    vars['delivery_day'] = eta.isoformat()\\n    vars['delivery_state'] = 'known'\\nexcept Exception:\\n    vars['delivery_day'] = ''\\n    vars['delivery_state'] = 'unknown'\\n\" },\n            \"timeout_ms\": { \"type\": \"number\", \"default\": 10000 }\n          }\n        }\n      }\n    }\n  ],\n  \"transitions\": [\n    { \"target_node\": \"tell_delivery\", \"description\": \"always\", \"condition\": { \"operator\": \"always\" } }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n| Parameter | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| `code` | The Python source, as a baked `default` string |\n| `timeout_ms` | Time limit in milliseconds. Default `30000` |\n\n### Reading and writing variables\n\n- The snippet gets a dict named `vars`. It holds the current workflow variables.\n- Read with `vars.get(\"name\")`. A variable that was never set is absent.\n- Write with `vars[\"name\"] = value`. Every key the snippet assigns becomes a workflow variable.\n\nVerified in a working builder:\n\n- Call variables are in `vars`. The builder reads `vars.get(\"now\")`, a call variable passed with the simulation.\n- The result of an earlier `api_request` node is reachable from `vars` in a later code node. The builder looks for it under the mapped variable name first and under the flattened result keys second. Which of the two held the data is not recorded.\n- A string written to `vars[\"date_facts\"]` in a code node resolves as `{{date_facts}}` in the `task_message` of later conversation nodes. Multi-line strings work.\n- The standard modules `json`, `re` and `datetime` can be imported.\n- The same snippet can run in two code nodes at different points of the call. The later run sees the variables added in between.\n\n### What a code node returns\n\nSeen in a trial call: the result of a successful `code_exec` is\n\n```json\n{ \"status\": \"success\", \"data\": null, \"message\": \"Code executed successfully\", \"status_code\": 200 }\n```\n\nThe snippet's output is not in `data`. Its values reach the workflow only through `vars`. After a code node named `setup` ran, the variable table held:\n\n| Variable | Value |\n|---|---|\n| each key the snippet wrote, for example `date_facts` | the value, with source `tool_result` and source node `setup` |\n| `setup_response.status` | `success` |\n| `setup_response.data` | `null` |\n| `setup_response.message` | `Code executed successfully` |\n| `setup_response.status_code` | `200` |\n| `setup_status_code` | `200` |\n| `setup_success_count` | `1` |\n\nFor a code node the automatic keys are named after the **node**, not after `code_exec`.\n\n### Rules for snippets\n\n- **Do not write `{{` or `}}` in the code.** The internal sources disagree on whether the `code` field is templated. One says it is exempt. Another says placeholders are resolved when the code runs and an unset one is left as literal text, which breaks the Python. The working builder removes every `{{` and `}}` from generated code (a nested dict literal such as `{'a': {'b': 1}}` ends in `}}`) and reads all values from `vars`.\n- **Never let a snippet raise.** Wrap the body in `try` and `except`, and write a fallback value for every variable that a later prompt or condition reads. A prompt that references an unset variable shows the literal placeholder to the model.\n- **Write strings for routing flags**, such as `known` and `unknown`. Do not route on a Python boolean. See \"Do not route on a boolean\" in workflow-format.md.\n- Keep snippets fast. They block the conversation.\n- Do arithmetic, date calculation and counting in code, and give the model the finished facts as text. Verified in a working builder: it computes a block of date facts in a code node and places it in the prompts.\n- Treat a code node like an API node: `passthrough: true`, a `passthrough_default_transition`, and a last `always` transition.\n\n### Sandbox limits\n\n| Limit | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Time | `timeout_ms`, default `30000` |\n| Modules | `json`, `re` and `datetime` are verified in a working builder. The full list is not documented |\n| Network and file access | Not documented |\n| Memory and output size | Not documented |\n\n### `code_exec` as a tool\n\n`code_exec` can also be a tool in the `tools` array of a conversation node, called by the model. Its parameters are top-level arguments, declared without `default` so the model fills them. For such a tool the `code` field resolves `{{var}}` when the tool is called, against the current variable table. The working builder does not use this form, so nothing about it is verified here.","numLines":370,"startLine":143,"totalLines":588}}}
